    "result_text": "Both tests pass and the full build is green (`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class; 2/2 passing). Let me give a final review of the change.\n\n## Summary\n\nI redesigned the owner identity key and duplicate detection to match the spec, keeping each piece of logic in its natural home.\n\n**Identity key (`Owner.getIdentityKey()`)** \u2014 now the SHA-256 hex digest of `normalizedTelephone + '|' + lowerEmail + '|' + soundex(lastName)`, replacing the old plaintext `telephone|email|householdId`. It reuses the stored (already E.164-normalized / lower-cased) fields, so it stays consistent with the values duplicate detection compares.\n\n**New shared helpers (model package, alongside `LuhnCheckDigit`/`FiscalYear`):**\n- `Soundex.of(...)` \u2014 the American Soundex algorithm (single source of truth).\n- `Sha256` \u2014 moved from `service` (was package-private) to `model` and made public, so both the entity and the existing `HouseholdIdGenerator`/`CustomerCodeGenerator` reuse one hashing implementation instead of duplicating it.\n\n**Duplicate detection (409):**\n- `ClinicService.isDuplicateOwner(owner)` returns true when a live owner shares the identity key. The controller calls it in `addOwner` *after* the email-domain blocklist (so a disposable-email duplicate is still a 400) and before persisting.\n- Deleted owners are ignored via a new repository query `findByDeletedFalse()` (added to the interface and all three impls \u2014 Spring Data, JPA, JDBC \u2014 for consistency).\n\n**Soft-match (`findPossibleDuplicate`)** \u2014 rewritten to trigger when the identity key *differs* but `soundex(lastName)` and postcode match, replacing the old household-id/different-telephone rule. Two owners with the same last name and postcode but different telephones now get soft-matched (different key) rather than hard-rejected.\n\nBoth checks share one candidate-loading helper, `liveOwnersSoundingLike(owner)`, which groups owners phonetically (the same basis the key uses) instead of by exact last name. Household-size and membership-cap logic (which legitimately use `householdId`) were left untouched. I also refreshed the stale Javadoc/OpenAPI descriptions for `identityKey` and `possibleDuplicate`.\n\nKey behaviors verified by `coreIdentityKeyIsSha256Hex` (64-hex shape) and `coreRejectsDuplicateIdentity` (identical identity \u2192 409), both passing.",
